@@ -0,0 +1,78 @@
+# Install Hive via your coding agent
+
+Paste everything below the horizontal rule into Claude Code, Codex, Pi, or any
+agent CLI that can run shell commands. The prompt is versioned with each hive
+release; pin it to a tag by linking
+`https://github.com/ivankuznetsov/hive/blob/v<version>/install.md`.
+
+The prompt is deliberately defensive: it detects the OS, picks the best native
+channel, verifies the result, and stops to ask before anything destructive.
+It never auto-installs dependencies beyond what the chosen channel needs, and
+it reports missing runtime tools with actionable hints instead of installing
+them silently.
+
+---
+
+You are installing Hive, an open-source multi-agent pipeline CLI, on this machine.
+
+Work through these phases in order. Stop and report at every stop-condition.
+
+## Phase 1 — Detect environment
+
+1. Run `uname -s` and `uname -m`. Supported tier-1 targets: macOS arm64,
+   Linux x86_64 (Ubuntu 22.04+, Arch). macOS x86_64 is tier-2 best-effort;
+   anything else: STOP and tell the user Hive does not package for their OS.
+2. Check `git`, `bash`, `claude` (Claude Code CLI), `gh` (GitHub CLI), `jq`.
+   For each missing tool print its official install hint:
+   - git: https://git-scm.com/downloads
+   - claude: `npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code`
+   - gh: https://cli.github.com/
+   - jq: system package manager or https://jqlang.github.io/jq/download/
+   Do NOT install them yourself unless the user explicitly asks.
+3. Check whether a command named `hive` already exists on PATH
+   (`command -v hive`). If it does and it is NOT hive-the-pipeline
+   (e.g. Apache Hive), remember this: use the fallback name `hv` everywhere
+   below instead of `hive`, and warn the user about the conflict.
+
+## Phase 2 — Choose the channel
+
+Pick exactly one, in this priority order:
+
+1. **Homebrew** if `brew` exists → run:
+   `brew install ivankuznetsov/hive/hive`
+2. **AUR helper** if on Arch Linux and `yay` or `paru` exists → run:
+   `<helper> -S hive-bin`
+3. **One-liner** otherwise → run:
+   `curl -fsSL https://github.com/ivankuznetsov/hive/releases/latest/download/install.sh | bash`
+
+STOP and ask before running any command that requires sudo.
+
+## Phase 3 — Verify
+
+Run `<hive-or-hv> --version`. It must print a semver like `0.1.x`.
+If it fails or the binary is not on PATH, diagnose using the channel's docs;
+do not fall back to a different channel without telling the user why.
+
+## Phase 4 — Initialize
+
+Ask the user which directory to initialize (default: current directory),
+then run `<hive-or-hv> init <dir>` inside it. Answer its interactive prompts
+with the user's choices; when the user has no preference, accept the defaults.
+Non-interactive? Use safe defaults and say so.
+
+## Phase 5 — Skills
+
+Hive's agent skills are distributed ONLY through each coding agent's own
+marketplace/plugin mechanism — never by the core binary.
+
+- If you are Claude Code: offer to run
+  `/plugin marketplace add <org>/hive-skills` and then install the hive plugin.
+- If your host supports an equivalent native marketplace flow, use it.
+- Otherwise tell the user the manual path from
+  https://github.com/ivankuznetsov/hive#skills and stop.
+
+## Report
+
+Summarize: detected OS/arch, chosen channel and why, installed version,
+`command -v <hive-or-hv>` output, initialized directory, missing optional
+dependencies with their hints, and the skills-install outcome.
